An effortless, high-protein breakfast and meal-prep favorite. Slices of deli ham crisp up along the edges inside a muffin tin, forming edible savory cups holding melted cheddar cheese and whole baked eggs. Whether you prefer jammy runny yolks or fully set eggs for grab-and-go weekday mornings, these bake in under 15 minutes.

*Adapted from [Delish](https://www.delish.com/cooking/recipe-ideas/recipes/a50901/ham-cheese-egg-cups-recipe/).*

## Ingredients

- Nonstick cooking spray, for coating muffin tin
- 12 thin round slices deli ham (Black Forest, Virginia, or honey ham)
- 1 cup shredded sharp cheddar cheese, divided
- 12 large eggs, cold
- ½ teaspoon kosher salt
- ¼ teaspoon freshly cracked black pepper
- 2 tablespoons fresh flat-leaf parsley, finely chopped (for garnish)

## Instructions

1. **Preheat and Grease Tin:** Heat your oven to 400°F (200°C). Spray a 12-cup standard metal muffin tin generously with nonstick cooking spray to prevent the ham from sticking.
2. **Shape the Ham Nests:** Take one slice of deli ham at a time and gently press it down into the center of each muffin cup. Pleat and ruffle the edges so the ham lines the bottom and climbs up the sidewalls, creating a neat cup with slightly protruding edges that will crisp in the oven.
3. **Layer the Cheese Base:** Divide half of the shredded cheddar cheese (about 1 tablespoon per cup) into the bottoms of the ham cups. This creates a gooey, melted buffer between the ham and the egg white.
4. **Crack Eggs and Season:** Crack one whole egg directly into each ham cup over the bed of cheese. Sprinkle each yolk and white evenly with kosher salt and freshly cracked black pepper. Scatter the remaining shredded cheddar cheese over the tops around the egg yolks.
5. **Bake to Preferred Doneness:** Slide the tin into the oven and bake at 400°F. Watch the bake time according to your yolk preference:
   - **Soft / Runny Yolks:** 11 to 12 minutes (whites just set, yolks molten).
   - **Medium / Jammy Yolks:** 13 to 14 minutes.
   - **Firm / Well-Done Yolks:** 15 to 16 minutes (ideal for meal prep and freezing).
6. **Rest and Unmold:** Remove from the oven and let the tin rest on a wire rack for 2 to 3 minutes so the steam subsides. Gently run a thin butter knife or offset spatula around the edges of each ham cup to release. Lift out and place on a warm serving platter.
7. **Garnish and Serve:** Garnish with freshly chopped parsley and serve warm with hot sauce, avocado slices, or toast.

## Notes

- **Meal Prep & Reheating:** Store cooled egg cups in an airtight container in the refrigerator for up to 4 days. Reheat on a microwave-safe plate on 50% power for 45 to 60 seconds.
- **Mix-In Ideas:** Add a pinch of diced red bell pepper, sautéed spinach, or sliced jalapeños beneath the egg for extra flavor and color.
